==English==

===Etymology===
{{unk|en}}, but possibly from {{m|en|brownnose}}, and often associated with the points that [[Girl Guide]]s or Girl Scouts at the {{w|Brownies (Scouting)|Brownie}} level (ages six through eight) receive for doing specific tasks that when accumulated earn them badges (a sign of recognition) to wear on their Brownie Uniform. First use appears c. 1944 in the ''{{w|The Daily Item (Sunbury)}}''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-brownie point.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|slang|originally|US|chiefly|in the plural}} [[credit|Credit]] or [[praise]] for good [[work]] or a good [[deed]], often for the express purpose of [[curry favor|currying favor]].
#: {{ux|en|You're really going to get some '''brownie points''' from the teacher for that fantastic essay!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1967|author=Harriette Bailey Conn|title=Women legislators|journal=Ebony|page=14|text=However, you get '''Brownie points''' for having spelled my names right—all of them, and the first time too!|month=November|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=xtsDAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA14}}

====Usage notes====
* This term is often used [[sarcastically]] to highlight someone's [[sycophantic]] behaviour.

